---
import ProjectLayout from '../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro';
import FlexyCanvas from '../../components/FlexyCanvas.astro';
---

<ProjectLayout slug="flexy" hint="Drag on the scene to draw a new arm">
  <FlexyCanvas slot="scene" />

  <section>
    <h2>What it does</h2>
    <p class="lead">Give it any Three.js geometry and a curve, and it bends the geometry to flow along that curve, keeping its cross-section intact.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The scene above</h2>
    <p>
      The lamp's arm is a plain, straight cylinder. Every frame, its original vertices are restored and
      <code>flexy.bend()</code> wraps them along a curve. When you drag, your stroke becomes that curve: it gets
      smoothed, turned into a spline, and the arm is re-bent live while you draw. The shade is oriented along the
      direction of the curve's last stretch, so it always points where the arm is heading.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <p>
      Each vertex's position along the geometry's long axis is mapped to a parameter <em>t</em> on the curve.
      flexy samples the curve's position and tangent at <em>t</em>, rotates the vertex's cross-section into the
      curve's local frame and writes the result back into the buffer. The deformation is smooth and continuous,
      with no pinching.
    </p>
    <div class="defs">
      <div class="def"><strong>fit</strong><span>Stretch or compress the geometry to cover the whole curve.</span></div>
      <div class="def"><strong>preserve</strong><span>Keep the original length and centre it on the curve.</span></div>
      <div class="def"><strong>tile</strong><span>Repeat the geometry along the curve when it's shorter than the arc.</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Usage</h2>
    <pre><code>{`import * as flexy from 'flexy-bend';

flexy.bend({
  THREE,
  curve,                       // any THREE.Curve
  bufferGeometry: mesh.geometry,
  axis: 'y',                   // the geometry's long axis
  orientation: new THREE.Vector3(0, 0, 1),
  mode: 'fit',
});`}</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Where you'd use it</h2>
    <ul class="list">
      <li>Cables, pipes, roads or rivers that follow a spline without manual rigging</li>
      <li>Procedural plants and vines that grow along a path</li>
      <li>Bending tails, tentacles or limbs along an animated curve</li>
      <li>Any mesh that needs to change shape in response to a moving path</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</ProjectLayout>
